# next-intl Rules

Multilingual support is part of the architecture, not a later feature.

**Use `next-intl` only.** Do not add or propose alternate i18n libraries (`react-i18next`, `next-i18next`, custom dictionary loaders, ad-hoc translation maps) unless a stricter project rule explicitly replaces this standard.

## Required stack

- App Router + `next-intl`
- Locale segment: `app/[locale]/...` (internal only; public URLs have no `/en` prefix — `localePrefix: "never"`)
- Locale config: `i18n/routing.ts` via `defineRouting`
- Server request config: `i18n/request.ts` via `getRequestConfig`
- Locale-aware navigation: project `i18n/navigation` helpers (`Link`, `redirect`, `useRouter`, `usePathname`)
- Message catalogs: `messages/{locale}.json` (supported: `en`, `bn`, `hi`, `or`; default `en`)
- Middleware/proxy wired for next-intl locale detection (`proxy.ts` on Next.js 16+) and unprefixed public URLs (`localePrefix: "never"`)

## Usage

- Server Components: `getTranslations` / `getLocale` from `next-intl/server`
- Client Components: `useTranslations` / `useLocale` from `next-intl`
- Prefer namespaced keys (`common.*`, `auth.*`, feature namespaces)
- Keep namespaces mirrored across all locale files
- Use ICU-style interpolation and pluralization from next-intl
- Use locale-aware date/time/number/currency formatting

## Content rules

- Never hardcode user-visible strings.
- Use translation keys for labels, buttons, placeholders, errors, validation messages, table headers, dialogs, notifications, tooltips, and accessibility labels.
- Do not concatenate translated strings.
- Do not assume English text length.
- Avoid fixed widths around translated text.
- Prefer CSS logical properties for directional spacing.
- Keep navigation labels as translation keys.
- Validate translation completeness in CI when possible.
- Missing translations must have an explicit fallback strategy (default locale via next-intl).
